## Proof

**Claim.** The function
$$f(x) = \frac{1}{|x|}, \quad x \neq 0, \qquad f(0) = 0$$
satisfies $f \notin L^{p}(\mathbb{R})$ for every $p > 0$, yet there exists $c > 0$ such that for all $t > 0$,
$$m\bigl(\{x \in \mathbb{R} : |f(x)| > t\}\bigr) \le \frac{c}{t}.$$

---

### 1. The weak-$L^{1}$ tail bound

The function $f$ is measurable (it is continuous on $\mathbb{R}\setminus\{0\}$, and the value at a single point is irrelevant for measure-theoretic purposes).

For any $t > 0$,
$$\{x \in \mathbb{R} : |f(x)| > t\} = \Bigl\{x \neq 0 : \frac{1}{|x|} > t\Bigr\} = \Bigl\{x : |x| < \frac{1}{t}\Bigr\}\setminus\{0\} = \Bigl(-\frac{1}{t},\,\frac{1}{t}\Bigr)\setminus\{0\}.$$

Since removing a single point does not change Lebesgue measure,
$$m\bigl(\{x : |f(x)| > t\}\bigr) = m\Bigl(\Bigl(-\frac{1}{t},\frac{1}{t}\Bigr)\Bigr) = \frac{2}{t}.$$

Thus the desired bound holds with **$c = 2$** (in fact with equality) for every $t > 0$.

---

### 2. $f \notin L^{p}(\mathbb{R})$ for any $p > 0$

We compute, for $p > 0$,
$$\int_{\mathbb{R}} |f(x)|^{p}\,dx = 2\int_{0}^{\infty} x^{-p}\,dx = 2\Biggl[\int_{0}^{1} x^{-p}\,dx + \int_{1}^{\infty} x^{-p}\,dx\Biggr].$$

**Near the origin** ($\int_{0}^{1} x^{-p}\,dx$): the antiderivative is $\frac{x^{1-p}}{1-p}$ (for $p \neq 1$), which diverges as $x \to 0^{+}$ when $1 - p \le 0$, i.e. when $p \ge 1$. For $p = 1$, $\int_{0}^{1} \frac{dx}{x} = +\infty$.

**Near infinity** ($\int_{1}^{\infty} x^{-p}\,dx$): the antiderivative $\frac{x^{1-p}}{1-p}$ diverges as $x \to \infty$ when $1 - p \ge 0$, i.e. when $p \le 1$. For $p = 1$, $\int_{1}^{\infty} \frac{dx}{x} = +\infty$.

Combining:

| Range of $p$ | $\int_{0}^{1} x^{-p}\,dx$ | $\int_{1}^{\infty} x^{-p}\,dx$ | Total |
|---|---|---|---|
| $0 < p < 1$ | finite ($=\frac{1}{1-p}$) | **diverges** | **diverges** |
| $p = 1$ | **diverges** | **diverges** | **diverges** |
| $p > 1$ | **diverges** | finite ($=\frac{1}{p-1}$) | **diverges** |

In every case the integral is $+\infty$, so
$$\int_{\mathbb{R}} |f(x)|^{p}\,dx = +\infty \quad \text{for all } p > 0.$$

Hence $f \notin L^{p}(\mathbb{R})$ for any $p > 0$.

---

### 3. Conclusion

The function $f(x) = 1/|x|$ (with $f(0)$ defined arbitrarily) belongs to **weak** $L^{1}(\mathbb{R})$, i.e. $L^{1,\infty}(\mathbb{R})$, with the distribution function satisfying $m(\{|f|>t\}) = 2/t \le 2/t$ for all $t > 0$, yet it fails to belong to $L^{p}(\mathbb{R})$ for **every** $p > 0$. This is consistent with the general fact that weak-$L^{1}$ is strictly larger than every $L^{p}$ with $p \ge 1$, and the slow decay $m(\{|f|>t\}) \sim 1/t$ at both small and large $t$ also prevents membership in $L^{p}$ for $0 < p < 1$ (via the layer-cake formula $\int|f|^{p} = p\int_{0}^{\infty} t^{p-1}\,m(\{|f|>t\})\,dt$, which diverges at $t\to\infty$ when $p<1$).

